 Three pivotal moments during World War I and their consequences:
1. The Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (June 28, 1914, Sarajevo, Bosnia): The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary and his wife, Sophie, by Gavrilo Princip, a member of the Serbian nationalist group known as the Black Hand, set off a chain reaction of events that led to the outbreak of war. The assassination provided Austria-Hungary with the pretext it needed to declare war on Serbia. Russia, which had promised to defend Serbia, responded by mobilizing its military, leading Germany to declare war on Russia and eventually involve other European powers in the conflict.
2. The Battle of the Marne (September 6-12, 1914, Marne River, France): The Battle of the Marne was a turning point in the Western Front of World War I. After invading France through Belgium, the German army had advanced deep into French territory, but the Allied forces, including the British Expeditionary Force and the French Army, stopped their advance at the Marne River. The battle marked the end of the German offensive and the beginning of trench warfare, which lasted until the end of the war. One lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome of the battle was the use of radio communication by the Allies, allowing them to coordinate their troops more effectively than the Germans.
3. The United States Enters the War (April 6, 1917): The United States remained neutral in World War I until April 1917, when it declared war on Germany after a series of events, including the interception of the Zimmermann Telegram, a message from Germany offering Mexico territory if it would attack the United States. The entry of the United States into the war gave the Allies a significant boost, as they had been struggling to maintain resources and manpower. The American Expeditionary Force played a key role in the final victory against Germany.
